  "notes": "PoE, read 2026-11-28. https://techspecs.ui.com/unifi/switching/usw-pro-max-16 (HTTP 200, Next.js spec table, text layer) prints **Port Layout = 12x 1G RJ45, 4x 2.5G RJ45, 2x 10G SFP+** - exactly the ports this file already carries - and **Max. Power Consumption 25W**, **Power Method AC/DC Adapter (included); Power Input Method USB Type-C, 5V DC, 5A; Power Supply AC/DC, external, 25W**. There is no PoE row on the sheet at all: no \"PoE Interfaces\", no per-port wattage, no \"Power over Ethernet\" budget - the whole PoE section that the PoE siblings print is absent, because this is the non-PoE Pro Max and it is powered from its own supply (a 25 W USB-C adapter). The PoE member of the family is a separate model with its own budget (USW-Pro-Max-16-PoE.json), so the absence is not an omission. The 16 copper ports therefore carry `poeIn: false`: without it `isPoePd()` in `engine/media.js` matched `unifi` in the label and inferred a 12.95 W 802.3af draw on every socket - **207.2 W** over 16 cables, asked of whatever PSE the lab cabled it to, on a box whose own supply is 25 W. Same correction as the non-PoE Switch Pro rows (PR #1712) and `uxg-pro.json` / `udm-pro-max.json` (PR #2111)."
